-- title:
--   Weighted centre: tame exponent, reduction, orbit support and transport
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime with $5 \le q$, $M'$ a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, and $\ell$ a prime with $3 \le \ell$, $\ell \ne q$ and $\ell \nmid M'$. Further data: a field $L$ of characteristic zero, an element $\xi \in L$ which is a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity, and the hypothesis `hι` that some ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ sends $\xi$ to $\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$; an intermediate field $K$ of $L \subseteq L(\!(t)\!)$ (Laurent series over $L$) which by `hK` equals [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.xHFunctionField ((q * ℓ) ^ 2 * M') (ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH (q * ℓ) M'))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the $L$-subfield of $L(\!(t)\!)$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the field of $q$-expansions over $\mathbb{Q}$ attached to $\Gamma_H \subseteq \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ for the level $N_0 = (q\ell)^2M'$ and the subgroup $H =$ `levelH (q * ℓ) M'`, the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/(q\ell)^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q\ell)^\times$ (the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q\ell$).
--
--   For the arithmetic of constants: $A$ is a domain which is a discrete valuation ring, henselian local, with algebraically closed residue field, equipped with an algebra structure on $L$ for which $L$ is its fraction field; `hAq` says $q \in \mathfrak{m}_A$ and `hξA` that $\xi$ lies in the image of $A$; $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. Next, $j$ is a nonzero element of $K$ whose Laurent expansion is the coefficientwise image in $L(\!(t)\!)$ of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) of the modular $j$-function; $\varpi$ generates $\mathfrak{m}_A$, and $\varpi_t \in A$ satisfies $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q u$ for some unit $u$ (the tame constant).
--
--   Write $C :=$ `chartAlgFin A ↥K j` for the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ consisting of the elements integral over $A[j]$, and `jChartFin A ↥K j` for $j$ viewed in $C$. The ideal $y \subseteq C$ is maximal, contains the image of $\varpi$, and satisfies the supersingularity hypothesis `hss`: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), the set of those $j_0 \in \Omega$ such that every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $j_0$ has no nonzero affine point killed by $q$.
--
--   On the geometric side, $X :=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A ↥K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) is the two-chart integral model, the pushout gluing $\operatorname{Spec} C$ and the spectrum of the algebra of elements integral over $A[j^{-1}]$ along the intermediate chart, with structure morphism `toBase` to $\operatorname{Spec} A$. A point $z$ of $X$ is given, together with the element $\varpi_z$ of the stalk $\mathcal{O}_{X,z}$ specified by `hϖz` as the germ at $z$ of the global section obtained from $\varpi$ through `toBase`, and `hz` places $\varpi_z$ in the maximal ideal of that stalk. A point $y'$ of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ is given with `ιFin` image $z$, satisfying the same supersingularity condition `hss'` for $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, and `hy'y` identifies $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal} = y$.
--
--   The formal chart data: $W_1$ is a domain which is a discrete valuation ring, complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal, $\sigma_1 : A \to W_1$ is a ring homomorphism with $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1} = (\sigma_1 \varpi)$, and $f_1, u_1, v_1 \in W_1[\![X_0,X_1]\!]$ with $u_1, v_1$ units and $f_1 \equiv X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ (the Drinfeld form [`DrinfeldCurve.LocalChart.drinfeldForm q W₁`](def/DrinfeldCurve_LocalChart.html#L18)). Finally $e_1$ is a ring isomorphism from the $\mathfrak{m}$-adic completion of $\mathcal{O}_{X,z}$ onto
--   $$S := W_1[\![X_0,X_1]\!]\big/\big(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{\,q+1})\,v_1 - f_1u_1\big).$$
--   Write $\mathrm{toC}$ for the canonical map from the stalk to its completion, $\mathrm{mk}_S$ for the quotient map onto $S$, and $\mathrm{germY} : C \to \mathcal{O}_{X,z}$ for the homomorphism obtained by identifying $C$ with the sections of $X$ over the image of the finite chart and taking germs at $z$; put $\Psi := e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY} : C \to S$ and $I := (\mathrm{mk}_S(\sigma_1\varpi_t), \mathrm{mk}_S X_0, \mathrm{mk}_S X_1) \subseteq S$, so that $J_y := \Psi^{-1}(I)$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hW₁` is a conjunction of seven clauses, which assert respectively: (i) constants — for $a \in A$ the image under $e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC}$ of the germ at $z$ of $a$ (through `toBase`) is $\mathrm{mk}_S$ of the constant $\sigma_1 a$; (ii) chart stability — for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and every $\tau \in \operatorname{Aut}_L(K)$ which is a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ in the sense of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L (q * ℓ) ξ (q * ℓ) ((q * ℓ) ^ 2 * M') (ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH (q * ℓ) M') γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) (namely: for every weight $k$, every pair $f,g$ of modular forms of weight $k$ for $\Gamma_H$ of level $(q\ell)^2M'$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g$, $p_g \ne 0$, and every $x \in K$ with Laurent series the image of $p_f/p_g$, the $\iota$-coefficientwise image of $\tau x$ times the $q$-expansion of $g\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}(q\ell)(\gamma^{-1})$ equals that of $f\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}(q\ell)(\gamma^{-1})$, for every $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ normalised by $\iota\xi = \exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$), the automorphism $\tau$ maps $C$ into $C$; (iii) for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M') \cap \Gamma(\ell)$ and such a $\tau$ preserving $C$, the induced endomorphism of $C$ is congruent to the identity modulo $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$; (iv) transport — for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, such a $\tau$ preserving $C$ and congruent to the identity modulo $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, there are a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, an element $c \in W_1$ and a matrix $M \in \mathrm{Mat}_2(W_1)$ with $\theta \circ \Psi = \Psi \circ \tau|_C$, with $\theta$ fixing all constants, with $\theta(\mathrm{mk}_S X_j) \equiv \sum_i \mathrm{mk}_S(M_{ij}X_i)$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by $\mathrm{mk}_S X_0, \mathrm{mk}_S X_1$, with $c^{q+1} \equiv 1$ and $M_{ij} \equiv c\,\gamma_{ij}$ modulo $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$, with $c \equiv 1$ modulo $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$ when $\gamma \in \Gamma(\ell)$, and with $c \not\equiv 1$ modulo $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$ when $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity; (v) separation of tangent directions — for integers $a_1,b_1,a_2,b_2$ and prime ideals $P_1,P_2$ of $S$, each omitting at least one of $\mathrm{mk}_S X_0,\mathrm{mk}_S X_1$, each containing $\mathrm{mk}_S(\sigma_1\varpi)$ and each containing an element $a_iX_0+b_iX_1+h$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, if $q \nmid a_1b_2-a_2b_1$ then the contractions of $P_1$ and $P_2$ along $e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC}$ are distinct; (vi) recognition — for a prime $P$ of $S$ omitting at least one of $\mathrm{mk}_S X_0, \mathrm{mk}_S X_1$, containing $\mathrm{mk}_S(\sigma_1\varpi)$ and containing $X_0 + h$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, an element $a \in C$ has $\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}\,a)$ in the contraction of $P$ along $e_1$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of $\mathfrak{m}_A$ in $L$; (vii) a witness at the $(q\ell)$-scaled $j$-expansion — the Laurent series [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L (q * ℓ)`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) (the $q$-expansion of $j$ after the substitution $t \mapsto t^{q\ell}$) lies in $K$ and in $C$, and there are $a_0 \in A$ with the difference of the two in $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, an integer $e_0 \ge 1$ and $h \in (X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ such that, first, for all $a,b \in W_1$ of which at least one is a unit and with $a^qb-ab^q \in \mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$, the sum $\sum_{i=0}^{e_0} \big(\text{coefficient of } X_0^iX_1^{e_0-i} \text{ in } h\big)a^ib^{e_0-i}$ is a unit of $W_1$, and second, $\Psi$ sends that difference to $\mathrm{mk}_S h$.
--
--   The last two data are: an ideal $J$ of $C$ which by `hJ` is the infimum of all ideals of the form $(\tau|_C)^{-1}(J_y)$, taken over $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and level automorphisms $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$ (the weighted centre); and an $A$-subalgebra $B$ of $K$ which by `hB` is the $C$-algebra generated inside $K$ by $\{x \in K \mid \exists\, i \in J,\ x\varpi_t = i\}$, viewed over $A$, that is $B = C[J/\varpi_t]$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses the conclusion is the conjunction of six assertions.
--
--   First: there exists $m \ge 1$ with $(\varpi_t) = \mathfrak{m}_A^{\,m}$ and $y^m \subseteq J_y$.
--
--   Second: for every $c \in y$ there exists $a \in C$ with $c - \varpi a \in J_y$.
--
--   Third: $J \subseteq J_y$.
--
--   Fourth: $J_y \subseteq y$.
--
--   Fifth: there exists $i_1 \in C$ with $i_1 - 1 \in y$ such that for every $c \in y$ there is $b \in B$ with $c\,i_1 = \varpi b$ in $K$.
--
--   Sixth: there exist a finite set $T$ of ideals of $C$ and an integer $N \ge 1$ such that $y \in T$, every $P \in T$ is maximal, $\prod_{P \in T} P^N \subseteq J$, and for every $P \in T$ there are $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and a level automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ over $L$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ which preserves $C$, such that $\tau^{-1}$ also maps $C$ into $C$, both $\tau$ and $\tau^{-1}$ map $B$ into $B$, and $P$ is the contraction of $y$ along the restriction of $\tau$ to $C$.
--
--   This collects the properties of the weighted blow-up centre $J$ at a supersingular point of the two-chart integral model of $X_H$: the tame exponent relating $(\varpi_t)$ to a power of $\mathfrak{m}_A$, the decomposition $y = \varpi C + J_y$, the inclusions $J \subseteq J_y \subseteq y$, local principality of $y$ in $B = C[J/\varpi_t]$, and the fact that the support of $J$ consists of finitely many maximal ideals, each carried to $y$ by a level automorphism which stabilises both $C$ and $B$. These are the inputs used by the subsequent lemmas that construct and analyse the blow-up chart $B$ and compare Drinfeld charts at linked points.
